  "content": {
    "id": "p38-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "Find a bijection $f$ from the set of even integers to the set of odd integers. What is the rule for the inverse of this bijection?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$f:2\\mathbb Z\\to 2\\mathbb Z+1$, $f(n)=n+1$.\n\n$f^{-1}:2\\mathbb Z+1\\to 2\\mathbb Z$, $f^{-1}(n)=n-1$.",
      "worked": "Define $f:2\\mathbb Z\\to 2\\mathbb Z+1$ by $f(n)=n+1$.\n\nIf $f(n)=f(m)$, then\n$$\\begin{align*}n+1&=m+1\\\\n&=m.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $f$ is injective.\n\nEvery odd integer is $2k+1$ for some $k\\in\\mathbb Z$, and $f(2k)=2k+1$. Thus $f$ is surjective and hence bijective.\n\nSolving $y=n+1$ gives $n=y-1$, so $f^{-1}:2\\mathbb Z+1\\to2\\mathbb Z$ is given by $f^{-1}(n)=n-1$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 45
  },
